#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# continue

#% text
# `continue()` gives `failure(continue)`, which ends the current step of the innermost iteration around it: a `while` or `for` loop goes on with its next iteration, and an iterating function such as `map`, `table`, `sum` or `select` leaves the element out.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Like every failure, `failure(continue)` ends the blocks and strict calls between it and the iteration.

#% item
# In `fold`, `nest` and `nest_while` the value stays as it was for that step.

#% item
# The iterating functions that read it are those listed on the page of `break`.

#% item
# Outside any iteration it stays a failure value.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
{ var s = 0; for k in range(10) { if is_odd(k) then continue(); s = s + k }; s }
#% output
#= 30

#% cell
sum(if is_odd(k) then continue() else k, [k, 1, 10])
#% output
#= 30

#% cell
map(x => if x < 0 then continue() else sqrt(x), [4, -1, 9])
#% output
#= [2, 3]

#% cell
total(select(range(10), is_even))
#% output
#= 30

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `break`, `catch`
